From 2397db5707eabcdb77352b0948a0f1019e5d9e92 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Ian Blenke <ian@blenke.com>
Date: Sat, 15 Aug 2026 10:02:29 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 09263/11362] Run the head-to-head on the config that actually
 lets both models finish

Operator directive 2026-08-15: "use both eGPUs to avoid the oom", then "I would
rather have head-to-head numbers first... focused on accuracy and success
regardless of how long it takes."

IT COMPLETES. On both cards at n_ctx 65536, Qwen3.8-27B induced tu93 in 1200s,
generating 41,613 tokens (~40k of them inline reasoning, split into
reasoning_content) and emitting 1,843 chars of COMPILING world model with real
mechanics: a player finder, a fuel bar draining from row 63, and a win condition
on cell (46,40) gated by fuel remaining. It terminated on its own. It was never
looping, and this is not the Qwen3.6 failure.

THE WHOLE FAILURE CHAIN WAS ONE NUMBER. tu93's induce_prompt is 15,771 tokens, so
n_ctx 32768 left 16,997 to answer in, and the four failed runs decoded 16,905 /
16,920 / 16,843 before being cut off -- `n_ctx - prompt` within 0.5%. Gemma fits
because its reasoning goes to a SEPARATE reasoning_content channel and does not
consume the answer window; Qwen3's is inline and does. The constant was never
neutral: it encoded an assumption about where a model puts its reasoning, and I
inherited it from the borrowed harness while calling the protocol untouched.

CHANGES, applied to BOTH arms so the comparison stays honest:
  n_ctx            32768 -> 65536 (~49.8k room to answer)
  topology         one card -> both, `-sm layer -ts 1,1`
  budget           symmetric N_CTX-16384; the per-arm ARM_BUDGET workaround is gone
  proposer timeout 1800 -> 3600s; the measured induction takes 1200s
  induce timeout   CARNOT_ARC_INDUCE_TIMEOUT=3600 (the 600s default would cut it dead)
  presence check   both UUIDs required -- losing either card is now fatal
  residency        SUMMED over both cards; per-card would see ~9.7 GB and wrongly
                   conclude the model never loaded

65536 does not fit on ONE 24 GB card: 17.8 GB model + KV + 32 context checkpoints
at ~150 MB dropped the connection mid prompt-processing. Split across two it uses
9.7 + 10.6 GB at 38.3 t/s -- no throughput cost, so speed stays comparable.

The abort-after-failed-qwen-arm guard is REMOVED. It was right when a failed arm
meant a broken config; now a partial qwen arm still needs gemma on the SAME config
or there is no comparison at all.

COST, accepted per the directive: ~20 min/cell for qwen against gemma's ~6, so
roughly 13h + 4h. ARC's metric squares efficiency, so this must be revisited
before any deployment claim -- but not now.

A LOSS I CAUSED, recorded: clearing the scratch with `rm -f h2h_shard_*.jsonl`
also matched the renamed failed shards I had deliberately set aside as evidence
(h2h_shard_qwen38_27b.CRASHED-RUN.jsonl and siblings). Their findings survive in
commit messages; the raw cells do not. Same glob-matched-more-than-intended class
as the six pkill self-kills today.
